WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.200
Today we are going to talk about image colorization.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:12.820
This is a problem where we take and old old
black and white photo, run it through one

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:19.160
of these amazing new learning-based algorithms,
and out comes an image that is properly colored.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:23.349
The obvious application of this is, of course,
image restoration.

00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:27.900
What you see here is some amazing results
with this new method, so clearly, this can

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.950
now be done very well, and you will find out
in a moment how challenging this problem is.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:38.950
But there are also, less obvious applications,
for instance, image compression too.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:43.940
Just imagine that we wouldn’t have to transmit
colored images over the internet, black and

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:49.680
white photos would be fine, and if there is
an AI-based algorithm in every computer, they

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.010
would be able to restore the colors perfectly.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:57.980
This would save a lot of bandwidth and energy
and would be a glorious thing to do.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:03.100
So, how well do the current learning-based
algorithms do at image colorization?

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:09.240
Wow, that is a hard, hard question, and, let’s
find out together why.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:15.100
First, let’s look at the results of this
new method that you saw so far against previously

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.260
existing techniques.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.830
Here is the black and white input image, and
here is the ground truth output that we have

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:23.420
concealed from the algorithms.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:28.300
Only we see the result, this will remain our
secret, and the algorithms only have access

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.159
to the black and white input.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:32.220
Let’s start.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:37.530
Yup, these are all learning based methods,
so they all appear to know what a strawberry

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.770
is, and they color it accordingly.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.340
So far so good.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:47.600
However, this problem is much, much harder
than just colorizing strawberries.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.170
We have grapes too!

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:53.590
That does not sound like much, but there are
many problems with grapes.

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:58.640
For instance, there are many kinds of grapes,
and they are also translucent, and therefore

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.420
their appearance depends way more on the lighting
in the scene.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:07.960
That’s a problem, because the algorithm
not only has to know what objects are present

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:12.980
in this image, but what the lighting around
them looks like, and how their material properties

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.529
should interact with this kind of lighting.

00:02:15.529 --> 00:02:19.670
Goodness, that is a tremendously difficult
problem.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:24.299
Previous methods did a reasonably good job
at colorizing the image, but the grapes remain

00:02:24.299 --> 00:02:25.670
a challenge.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:30.329
And now, hold on to your papers and let’s
see the new method!

00:02:30.329 --> 00:02:33.379
Wow, just look at that!

00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:38.269
The translucency of the grapes is captured
beautifully, and the colors are very close

00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:39.709
to the ground truth.

00:02:39.709 --> 00:02:42.440
But that was just one example.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.370
What if we generate a lot of results and show
it to a few people.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:47.959
What do the users say?

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:51.859
Let’s compare the DeOldify technique with
this new method.

00:02:51.859 --> 00:02:57.540
DeOldify is an interesting specimen as it
combines three previously published techniques

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.730
really well, but it is not a published paper.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:05.200
Fortunately, its source code is available,
and it is easy to compare results against

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.200
it.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:09.609
So let’s see how it fares against this new
technique!

00:03:09.609 --> 00:03:14.280
Apparently, they trade blows, but more often
than not, the new method seems to come out

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:15.569
ahead.

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:17.889
Now note that we have a secret.

00:03:17.889 --> 00:03:23.300
And that secret is that we can see the reference
images, which are hidden from the algorithms.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:28.030
This helps us a great deal, because we can
mathematically compare the results of the

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:30.640
learning algorithms to the reference.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.060
So what do the numbers say?

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:38.419
Yup, now you see why I said that it is very
hard to tell which algorithm is the best,

00:03:38.419 --> 00:03:43.569
because they all trade blows, and depending
on how we measure the difference between two

00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:46.230
images, we get a different winner.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:51.519
All this is true… until we have a look at
the results with the new paper.

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:56.559
If you have been holding on to your papers,
now squeeze that paper, because this new method

00:03:56.559 --> 00:04:02.439
smokes the competition on every dataset, regardless
of what we are measuring.

00:04:02.439 --> 00:04:04.780
So what is this black magic?

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:06.839
What is behind this wizardry?

00:04:06.839 --> 00:04:12.099
This method uses an off-the-shelf object detection
module that takes the interesting elements

00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:16.030
out of the image, which are then colorized
one by one.

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:20.759
Of course, this way, we haven’t colorized
the entire image, so parts of it would remain

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:24.800
black and white, which is clearly not what
we are looking for.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.479
As a remedy, let’s color the entire image
independently from the previous process.

00:04:30.479 --> 00:04:36.110
Now we have an okay-quality result where the
colors have reached the entirety of the image,

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:38.250
but now, what do we do?

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:43.819
We also colorized the objects independently,
so some things are colorized twice, and they

00:04:43.819 --> 00:04:44.819
are different.

00:04:44.819 --> 00:04:51.259
This doesn’t make any sense whatsoever…until
we introduce a novel fusion module to the

00:04:51.259 --> 00:04:56.360
process, that stitches these overlapping results
into one coherent output.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.099
And the results are absolutely amazing.

00:04:59.099 --> 00:05:02.180
So how much do we have to wait to get all
this?

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:07.960
If we take a small-ish image, the colorization
can take place five times a second, and just

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:12.810
two more papers down the line, this will easily
run in real time I am sure.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:17.490
You know what, with the pace of progress in
machine learning research today, maybe even

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:19.900
just one paper down the line.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:22.300
So, what about the limitations?

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:27.210
You remember that this contains an object
detector, and if it goes haywire, all bets

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:33.650
are off and we might have to revert to the
only okay full-image colorization method.

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:39.900
Also, note that all of these comparisons showcased
image colorization, while DeOldify is also

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.810
capable of colorizing videos as well.

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.639
But of course, one step at a time.

00:05:45.639 --> 00:06:41.289
What a time to be alive!

00:06:41.289 --> 00:06:44.539
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

